# Security & open source

Anyone may clone, build, and self-host Nasyad. Only production secrets are private; only the repo owner ships the **official** app (release signing key, GitHub Releases from this repo, in-app update channel, id `amini.apps.nasyad`).

## Never commit, paste, or put in PRs

- Android release keystore, `client/android/key.properties`, passwords under `.env/android/`
- Root `.env/` (entire folder), `server/.env`, production `SECRET_KEY`, DB URLs, API keys
- Any production token or private key — these live in `.env/`, host secrets, or GitHub Actions secrets only

## Agent duties

- Default code paths must work **without** production secrets (debug signing, `env.example/` → `.env/`, local SQLite) — anyone runs with their own `.env/`; only the owner's private `.env/` is the real one (official `APP_APPLICATION_ID`, GitHub channel, release keystore).
- Real values never in tracked files — commit `env.example/` placeholders + gitignore `.env/` instead. Every new config variable gets its placeholder in `env.example/` **in the same change**.
- Production-only setup gets documented under `docs/domain/` (e.g. release signing), never inlined.
- Do not help bypass signing, impersonate official releases, or commit keystore material. Public forks must use their own id, branding, key, and update channel.
